  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>Retail health and safety sits under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974 and the Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999</li><li>Slips and trips are the most common cause of major injury in retail, so escape routes, spillages and obstructions lead the walk</li><li>Fire exits and escape routes fall under the Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005; certain incidents are reportable under RIDDOR 2013</li><li>Most area managers record the walk on paper and type it up later from memory, and the hazard record suffers</li><li>The checklist does not make the store safe; fixing what it finds does, and the tool flags hazards to head office as they happen</li></ul></div><h2>What the retail safety walk is for</h2><p>A store health and safety inspection is a structured walk that checks a shop against its duties under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974 and the Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999. It covers the customer-facing floor, the stockroom and back-of-house, and the safety records the store keeps. It is usually run by the store manager as a routine check and by the area or regional manager on their visits.</p><p>The checklist does not make the store safe. Fixing what it finds does. What a good tool does is make the hazard easier to record at the point it is seen, harder to forget, and faster to escalate to someone who can act. This page is general information, not legal advice; check the current regulations and your own risk assessments.</p><h2>The store safety walk, section by section</h2><h3>1. Shop floor: slips, trips and escape routes</h3><p>Slips and trips are the single most common cause of major injury in retail, so the floor leads the walk. The check looks for spillages and wet floors without signage, trailing leads, obstructed aisles, damaged flooring and trip edges, and stock left in customer routes. It confirms that fire exits and escape routes are clear, unlocked and signed under the Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005.</p><h3>2. Back of house: stockroom and manual handling</h3><p>The stockroom carries unstable stacking, damaged racking, ladders and kick-steps in poor condition, and manual handling of heavy loads under the Manual Handling Operations Regulations 1992. The walk checks safe stacking heights, access equipment and whether staff can move stock safely.</p><h3>3. Records: fire log and accident reporting</h3><p>The records check confirms fire alarm and extinguisher checks are current, first-aid provision is stocked, the accident book is kept, and reportable incidents are handled under RIDDOR 2013. Each record check is captured as evidence with a timestamp.</p><h2>Hazards that escalate, not just file</h2><p>The point of running the same safety walk across every store is to see the pattern and to catch the urgent thing fast. A blocked fire exit or an unguarded spillage should reach someone who can act while the manager is still in the store, not wait for a report. Quickler flags hazards to head office the moment the manager records them and surfaces every completed safety walk on one dashboard, with failed items and open actions visible across the estate. The record carries a timestamp, the manager, the store and every observation, so an insurer, an HSE inspector or a Primary Authority partner can follow exactly what was checked and when.</p><h2>What to look for in a safety inspection tool</h2><ul><li><strong>On-site completion:</strong> the tool should make it easier to record the hazard during the walk, not afterwards</li><li><strong>Photo evidence at the point of observation:</strong> each hazard tied to a timestamped image, not described from memory later</li><li><strong>Real-time hazard flagging:</strong> a blocked exit or live spillage should reach head office as it is recorded, not a week later</li><li><strong>No per-seat penalty:</strong> per-seat pricing punishes a retail estate for its store and head-office staff. Quickler charges £20 per active user per month with dormant users free</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> timestamp, manager, store, every observation, for the insurer, HSE inspector or Primary Authority partner who asks later</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the safety walk and the evidence and flags hazards to head office as they happen. It does not make a store safe. The store gets safer when the actions get closed. This page is general information, not legal advice; check the regulations and risk assessments that apply. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What does a retail health and safety inspection cover?</h3><p>It covers the shop floor, the stockroom and back-of-house, and the store's safety records. On the floor: slips, trips, spillages, obstructions and clear fire exits. In the back: safe stacking, access equipment and manual handling. In the records: fire checks, first aid and accident reporting. It is run against the store's duties under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974.</p><h3>How often should a store do a health and safety walk?</h3><p>There is no single statutory frequency; it depends on the store's own risk assessment. In practice many retailers run a manager check daily or weekly for the obvious hazards, plus a fuller area-manager inspection on each store visit. Check your own health and safety policy and risk assessments for the frequency that applies.</p><h3>What laws apply to health and safety in a retail store?</h3><p>The main duties come from the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974 and the Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999. Fire safety falls under the Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005, manual handling under the Manual Handling Operations Regulations 1992, and reportable incidents under RIDDOR 2013. This is general information, not legal advice; check the current regulations that apply.</p><h3>Can I run a store safety inspection over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's safety walk runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The manager receives each question in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo of any hazard, and the completed report generates automatically. Hazards flag to head office the moment they are recorded. No separate app or login is required, and quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/retail-store-audit-app-uk.html\">Retail store audit app for multi-site estates</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/merchandising-compliance-audit-uk.html\">Merchandising compliance audit</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/food-retail-hygiene-audit-uk.html\">Food retail hygiene audit</a></li></ul>",
